[h1]SaucedCarts v2.1.16[/h1]

[b]Fixed: an error on startup after the latest game patch.[/b]
The latest patch changed how the game reads percent signs in mod text. A plain % is now taken as a placeholder for a number to be filled in rather than as a percent sign, and ours appeared twice in the Weight Reduction sandbox setting — once in its name, once in its description. The description produced a warning the game shrugs off; the name produced an error it does not, which is where the stack trace came from. Both are corrected. If you are seeing errors like this from other mods you have installed, it is almost certainly the same cause, and it needs the same small fix in each of them.

[b]Fixed: carts left on the ground being deleted by server world cleanup.[/b]
Many servers periodically sweep away items left lying around. The game normally spares anything a player put down by hand — that exemption is the reason your dropped belongings survive while spawned clutter does not. Carts were never being marked as player-dropped, which made them the one thing you could set down that cleanup would still take, and if a cart broke, everything that spilled out of it was just as unprotected. Carts and their contents are now marked the same way as anything else you drop. This does not cover a cart kept inside a vehicle. If you have had one vanish from a trunk, please say so and mention what your server uses for cleanup, because nothing in the base game is able to empty a vehicle.

[b]Fixed: items vanishing when moved between a cart and a bag stored inside a vehicle.[/b]
If you kept a backpack or duffel in a trunk or truck bed and moved things straight between it and a cart, the transfer looked like it never happened, and the items were gone the next time that bag re-synced — usually the moment you took it back out of the vehicle. The move was actually being carried out correctly the whole time; the game simply had no way to tell anyone about it. A bag sitting inside a vehicle is a container inside a container, and the message that says "this item moved" had nowhere to address itself, so it was thrown away without any error. Loading a cart, the disappearance from the bag never arrived, so the item seemed to stay put. Unloading a cart, the arrival never came, so it seemed to evaporate. Both directions are fixed, the correction now reaches every player nearby rather than just the one doing the moving, and an open container window keeps working instead of going stale. Bags inside furniture like shelves and crates were affected the same way and are also fixed, though there the container window may need a moment to catch up.

[b]Fixed: not being able to load a cart you are pushing once it gets heavy.[/b]
Past a certain point, moving anything into a cart you had equipped would quietly refuse — from a trunk, a bag, a shelf or a corpse — even with plenty of space left in the cart. The cart's weight counts toward what your character is carrying, so the fuller it got, the more overloaded you looked, and the game was requiring the item to fit [i]you[/i] rather than the cart. That is sensible for a backpack you are wearing and backwards for something you are wheeling along, so carts are now exempt. The cart's own capacity is still the limit, and still enforced. Nothing was logged when this happened, which is a large part of why it took a report to find; it is logged now.

[b]Fixed: a cart equipped with "Take in both hands" looking like a held weapon to other players.[/b]
Using the vanilla both-hands option instead of Push Cart worked, but everyone else saw the cart carried like a weapon and clipping through the ground. The pushing pose is now announced whenever a cart ends up in your hands, however it got there. The duplicate vanilla option is also properly hidden now on non-English clients, where it had been slipping through.

[b]Fixed: corpses loaded into a cart still lying on the ground for everyone else.[/b]
This one only showed up with more than one player around. You would grab a body, load it into your cart, and to you everything looked right — but anyone else nearby still saw the corpse where you picked it up, and it stayed there for good. Worse, it kept counting as a body for them, so it went on making them sick and drawing flies long after it had been carted away. The cause was ours. When a corpse is dragged the game quietly swaps it for a stand-in, and that stand-in is the only thing that remembers which body to tidy up afterwards. We were clearing the stand-in away the instant the corpse went into the cart, before the game had a chance to use it, so on any machine that had not already tidied up the body was left behind with nothing left to identify it. We now wait about a second before clearing the stand-in, which is all the game needed. Verified with two players against a dedicated server.

No save or database changes, so this is safe to apply mid-run. Thanks to everyone who reported these with the specific setup that triggered them — "a bag inside the trunk" was the detail that made the first one findable, and the hunch about the cart being nearly full turned out to be a second, separate bug sitting right next to it.
